course: dictionary
lesson: 12150
title: Compelling words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Compelling is an adjective. It tells you that something is so strong, interesting or convincing that you cannot stop watching, reading or believing it. «The mystery story was so compelling that Priya read it under her blanket with a torch.» She could not stop reading, so compelling means very gripping.
explain: Look at the words next to it for clues. «The scientist showed compelling evidence that the river was getting cleaner.» Evidence is proof, and the word compelling tells you the proof is strong enough to convince everyone. Test it: swap in very strong or very convincing. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Compelling also comes from the verb compel, which means to force someone to do something. «The heavy rain compelled us to stay indoors.» The rain forced us to stay inside. You can spot the verb by its other forms: compel, compels, compelled, compelling.
fact: Compelling means so interesting or exciting that you want to keep reading or watching.
fact: «Compelling evidence» means proof that is strong enough to make you believe something.
fact: «A compelling reason» is a reason so strong that you have to agree with it.
fact: Compel is a verb that means to force someone to do something, so «compelled» is its past tense.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple word such as gripping, strong or convincing and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The detective gave a compelling reason to think the butler was innocent.» What does compelling mean here? | Hidden under the bed | Very weak and easy to doubt | *Strong enough that you have to believe it | Very loud and noisy
exercise: «The book was so compelling that the whole class went quiet.» What does compelling suggest about the book? | *So interesting that nobody wanted to stop listening | So boring that everyone fell asleep | Short and rude | Written in pencil
exercise: «The heavy rain ___ us to stay indoors.» Which word fits the gap? | Barely | Compelling | *Compelled | Ancient
test: «The teacher showed compelling evidence that the plant needed more sunlight.» What does compelling mean here? | Slow and quiet | *Strong enough to be convincing | Very colourful | Weak and unconvincing
test: Sam says, «The film was so compelling I forgot to eat.» What does Sam mean? | Sam was cold | The film was boring | The film was too short | *The film was so gripping that Sam could not stop watching
exam: «Her argument was compelling, so the judge agreed with her.» Which word means the same as compelling here? | Silent | *Convincing | Dull | Confusing
